The '''Republic of Mazar''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Jamhuuriyadda Mazar'') was a {{W|merchant republic}} centred on the city of [[Mazar]], located on a peninsula on the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast of [[Southwestern Kaftia]], approximately 50 km southwest of the mouth of the [[Buran River]]. Governed by an oligarchic council of wealthy trading families, Mazar was the principal port for the export of [[Kushish]] highland iron to the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world and a major centre of [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] and [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] commercial activity. The republic was dissolved in 403 AD when '''[[Appa of Mazar]]''' married '''[[Bugad the Great]]''', prince of the neighbouring [[Kingdom of Iskhal]], uniting both polities into the [[Kingdom of Burawa]].

== Origins ==

=== Founding myth ===
The cities of [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] share a common founding tradition. According to this account, twin sons of an ancient king who ruled the coastal settlements near the lower [[Buran River]] were each given a domain: the son who "touched the sea" received the coastal peninsula and was named '''Mazar'''; the son who "touched the sky" received the inland territory and was named '''Iskhal'''. The '''Iskhal River''', a minor coastal stream, marked the boundary between the two domains.

=== Early history ===
The coastal settlements near the lower [[Buran River]] were among the sites brought under [[Kingdom of Ufyen|Ufyen]] tributary rule during the '''[[Dagaalka Buran]]''' (~700–650 BCE). Following the [[Ancient Southwestern Kaftia#Geedi collapse|Geedi collapse]] (~500 BCE), the community divided along the lines described in the founding myth: the inland settlement became the city-state of Iskhal, while the peninsula settlement retained the name Mazar. The arrival of [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] traders around 600 BCE, who established trading posts at the natural harbour on the peninsula, accelerated Mazar's development as a commercial centre.

Unlike Iskhal and most other [[Ancient Burawi city-states]], Mazar did not develop hereditary rule. Instead, the city's government took the form of an oligarchic republic dominated by the merchant families who controlled the Mesogean trade.

== Geography ==

Mazar occupied a rocky peninsula projecting into the [[Mesogean Sea]], connected to the mainland by a narrow isthmus. The peninsula's natural harbour, sheltered from prevailing winds on the eastern side, made it one of the best anchorages on the southern coast. The city's landward side was bounded by the '''Iskhal River''' and, beyond it, the territory of the [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]] polity.

This geography shaped Mazar's character. The peninsula provided natural defences against land-based attack but limited the city's territorial expansion. Mazar was compact, densely populated, and oriented entirely toward the sea. Over time, as [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]] expanded across the Iskhal River and surrounded the peninsula on its landward sides, Mazar became increasingly dependent on its maritime connections for economic survival.

== Government ==

The Republic of Mazar was governed by a '''Council of Merchants''' (''Golaha Ganacsatada''), an assembly of the heads of the wealthiest trading families. The council elected a presiding magistrate — the '''Suldaan''' — from among its members to serve as the republic's chief executive and representative in foreign affairs. The position was not hereditary; it was filled through election by the council, though in practice a small number of families dominated the office across generations.

Below the council, a class of lesser merchants, shipowners, and artisan guilds participated in civic affairs through neighbourhood assemblies, though real power was concentrated among the trading families who controlled the iron export trade and the relationships with [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] and [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] commercial firms.

== Economy ==

Mazar's wealth derived from its position as the primary point of contact between the [[Southwestern Kaftia|Kaftian]] interior and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] trading world. Iron ingots, copper, and hardwood timber from the [[Kushish]] highlands — transported down the [[Buran River]] and overland through [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]] territory — were loaded onto Mesogean and Mazari-built vessels at the peninsula harbour for export across the Mesogean.

In return, Mazar imported gold, silver, wine, olive oil, fine ceramics, and other luxury goods from [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] and [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] sources. The city's harbour district housed permanent trading offices of both Aetolian and Volisanian firms, and shrines to the Aetolian deities '''[[Talnos]]''' (sea and navigation) and '''[[Zilath]]''' (trade and fortune) were maintained alongside the civic temple of [[Waaq]].

The republic minted its own coinage from Mesogean silver, and Mazari coins circulated widely along the southern coast. The city also maintained a substantial merchant fleet, built in local shipyards using a combination of indigenous construction methods and Mesogean hull designs.

== Relations with Iskhal ==

The relationship between Mazar and the [[Kingdom of Iskhal]] was shaped by the geographic interdependence of the two cities. Mazar controlled the harbour and the fleet; Iskhal controlled the land routes and the overland approaches. Neither city could conduct the iron trade without the other's cooperation.

This complementarity produced periods of both collaboration and friction. Trade agreements and shared customs arrangements alternated with disputes over revenue, tariff rates, and territorial boundaries. Iskhal's gradual expansion across the Iskhal River — which by the 4th century AD had surrounded the Mazar peninsula on three of its four sides — placed the republic under increasing pressure. Mazar retained freedom of action at sea but was dependent on Iskhal's consent for overland trade and food supply.

== The Mazar Clique and the founding of Burawa ==
{{main|Kingdom of Burawa}}

By the late 4th century AD, the governing families of Mazar had concluded that the republic's long-term commercial ambitions required a political arrangement beyond the existing city-state structure. The council established a '''ruling family''' — later known as the '''[[Mazar Clique]]''' or '''House of Masar''' — with the specific purpose of forging a dynastic alliance with the [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]] monarchy.

In '''403 AD''', '''[[Appa of Mazar]]''', a member of this family, married '''[[Bugad the Great]]''', prince of the [[Urtog Dynasty]] of Iskhal. The marriage united the two polities under a single crown, creating the '''[[Kingdom of Burawa]]'''. The republican institutions of Mazar were formally dissolved, though the Mazar Clique continued to operate within the new kingdom as an influential network connecting the crown to the merchant class.

The Clique's influence persisted for generations. Following the death of '''Dalgayan II''' in 573 AD, the Mazar Clique secured the succession of '''[[Iacob I]]''', the late king's great-nephew, to the throne. The family's hold on the monarchy continued until the accession of '''[[Marcel I]]''' of '''[[House Kadim]]''', which established a new ruling dynasty.

